---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Never load the whole folder — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB of memory and crashed. The fix is the idea I reuse most in the code, and I've now made the same mistake four times."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-01">September 1, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Never load the whole folder</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB of memory and then crashed. The
          fix is the idea I reuse most in the code, and I've now made the same
          mistake four times, each time in a different spot.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          LightPhotos crashed on a folder of 8,889 photos because I was loading
          a thumbnail for every single one. I fixed it by only keeping what's on
          screen, plus a little extra. Then I found the same mistake three more
          times in other parts of the app. If you're building anything that
          handles someone's photo library, check where your code quietly
          assumes the folder is small.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Look for loops over everything the user gave you.</strong>
          Loops that handle one request are usually fine. The ones that grow
          with how many photos someone owns are the ones that bite.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Size memory by what has to be kept at once.</strong> I kept
          asking how many photos were in a job. The better question was how
          long each thumbnail actually needs to stay in memory.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the calculation next to the number.</strong> Whoever
          reads a bare 32 later, including me, needs to know what would make it
          wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Recheck old numbers when things change.</strong> Three of
          these four bugs were correct when I wrote them. Something changed
          later and I didn't go back.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Imagine a wedding photographer handing you a card with 8,889 photos on
        it. You open the folder in LightPhotos, and the app dies.
      </p>
      <p>
        That was a real bug. I didn't expect to fix the same bug three more
        times, in places that looked nothing like each other.
      </p>

      <h2>The crash</h2>
      <p>
        The grid shows a small preview (a thumbnail) for every photo in the
        folder. My code for filling it came down to this:
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">for each photo in folder:
    request a thumbnail
    upload it to the GPU as a texture</pre>
      <p>
        That loop is fine if you have twenty photos. With 8,889 it asks the
        graphics card to hold 8,889 images (textures), about 1.3 GB of video
        memory, which pushes the app's total memory use to around 1.9 GB. Then
        the operating system kills the app.
      </p>
      <p>
        The loop itself does what it says. It just assumes a folder only has a
        few photos in it, and I never noticed I was assuming that.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the first time</h2>
      <p>
        On a large monitor you can see about forty thumbnails at once. The
        other 8,849 are off screen and nobody needs them yet.
      </p>

